#{[17592186525010 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525010 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525010 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525010 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $5$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525010 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fd741b4-3eaf-54db-b2d4-05c07c61f572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525010 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{3}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{5}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{3}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{5}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{5}={x}^{3+5}={x}^{8}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525034 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525034 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525034 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $({x}^{2}-3x)+({x}^{3})$\n\nII. $(1-2x)-(2-{x}^{2})$\n\nIII. $(x+1)\\cdot (4-{x}^{2})$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc096998-81ea-5954-87f7-a6c387f0821d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525034 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525045 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $6$, then what is the **smallest possible** degree of $P-Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4519b02-5082-5dd4-add0-c15242596fca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525045 #edb/ref 1074 "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{6}$ and $Q={x}^{6}+1.$ Then $P-Q=-1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525054 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525054 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525054 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $5$, respectively, what is the degree of $P+Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e218d969-ef9b-5d39-ab4e-db60fd086832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525054 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{4}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{5}.$\n\nIn $P+Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{5}.$ No ${x}^{5}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{5}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must be $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
